<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rock of Cashel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cashel of the Kings</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rock of Cashel is a limestone outcrop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-tipperary">County Tipperary</a>, Ireland, that was the seat of the kings of Munster from about the 4th century. In 1101 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-ua-briain">Muirchertach Ua Briain</a> gave it to the Church. Its buildings include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cormacs-chapel">Cormac's Chapel</a>, a Romanesque church consecrated in 1134, a round tower and a 13th-century cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rock-of-cashel.jpg" alt="Rock of Cashel" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_of_Cashel_(49163525453).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
